================================================================================
=  VIMTUTOR ADVANCED  --  Lesson 12: Completion and LSP with coc.nvim         =
================================================================================

  coc.nvim is the bridge between your editor and language servers
  (LSP). One Node.js-based setup serves BOTH Vim and Neovim with the
  same keys: popup completion, live diagnostics, hover docs, rename,
  code actions. setup.sh installed Node for you; plugins install via
  :CocInstall (Lesson 9).

  1. Completion in practice:
     ~
     ~   :e demo/lang-demo.js<Enter>     the scratch copy of the demo
     ~   :cd %:p:h                       make the demo dir the workspace
     ~                                   root (coc infers it from cwd)
     ~
     ~  Inside the demo file, after "console." -- just start typing:
     ~
     ~   console.l       the menu pops by itself (it is tsserver talking)
     ~   If nothing pops:  Ctrl-Space forces the trigger
     ~
     ~   Inside the popup menu:
     ~     Ctrl-N Ctrl-P    next / previous item (works in any popup)
     ~     Tab              accept (and expand snippets, if any)
     ~     Ctrl-y  /  Ctrl-e   accept / reject with a single key
     ~
     ~  Complete .log( then watch the floating signature docs. Insert
     ~  mode navigation: the menu is a normal part of editing -- no
     ~  mode change to reach it.

  2. Diagnostics -- errors as you type:
     ~
     ~   :CocList diagnostics<Enter>     every problem, searchable list
     ~     j k to move, <Enter> to jump, q to close the list
     ~   ]g  /  [g                        next / previous diagnostic
     ~
     ~  The signs in the sign column next to line numbers are coc's
     ~  live diagnostics (E/W/I). They turn on with the plugin -- no
     ~  extra config. :CocList diagnostics is the searchable window
     ~  over that same data.
     ~
     ~  (In the demo, line-length or unused-variable warnings only
     ~  appear once the tsserver extension is around: :CocInstall
     ~  coc-tsserver -- Lesson 9 step 4.)

  3. Hover, definition, references -- the navigation core:
     ~
     ~   K               hover documentation for the symbol at cursor
     ~   gd              go to DEFINITION (and the jumplist knows!)
     ~   Ctrl-O          ...come back home through the same trail
     ~   gr              list all REFERENCES (a location list opens)
     ~   gy / gi         type definition / implementation
     ~
     ~  Try in the demo: K on console.log, gd into its declaration,
     ~  Ctrl-O back, gr on announce to see both call sites.

  4. Rename and code actions -- safe refactoring:
     ~
     ~   <leader>rn      RENAME across the workspace (your config maps it)
     ~   <leader>ac      CODE ACTION at cursor: fixes, refactors
     ~   <leader>qf      the QUICKFIX action when a diagnostic offers one
     ~   (your shared config defines these; :nmap <leader>rn to verify)
     ~
     ~  In the demo: rename announce with <leader>rn, type a new name,
     ~  Enter -- both files' references follow, because it is the
     ~  language server doing it, not a regex.

  5. The lists and commands surface:
     ~
     ~   :CocList                open the list picker (outline, symbols...)
     ~   :CocList outline        the file's structure as a tree
     ~   :CocCommand             everything searchable: try
     ~                           :CocCommand editor.action.formatDocument
     ~   :CocConfig              opens coc-settings.json -- JSON, not vimrc
     ~   :CocRestart             when a server sulks
     ~   :CocInfo                version + diagnostics for bug reports

  6. Language servers per language (the usual suspects):
     ~
     ~   :CocInstall coc-tsserver        javascript / typescript
     ~   :CocInstall coc-json            json (used for :CocConfig itself)
     ~   :CocInstall coc-pyright         python
     ~   :CocInstall coc-clangd          C/C++ (auto-installs clangd)
     ~   :CocList extensions             what is installed right now
     ~
     ~ NOTE: differences worth knowing. In Neovim you may meet configs
     ~ based on nvim built-in LSP + nvim-cmp instead of coc; the DATA is
     ~ the same, only keys/config differ. This tutor standardizes on coc
     ~ so keys match across both editors. Also: coc needs a modern Node
     ~ (18+). If popups never appear, :CocInfo and :checkhealth will say.

  7. Scala 3 with Metals -- a JVM language, same keys:
     ~
     ~  Metals is the Scala language server. Its old coc extension is
     ~  dead, so your config hands coc the metals launcher directly
     ~  (:CocInfo lists it as "metals"). It needs a one-time install:
     ~
     ~   sh setup.sh --with-scala        Coursier + Metals + scala-cli,
     ~                                   plus a JDK 17+ only if none is
     ~                                   found (sdkman's comes first)
     ~
     ~  Then, from this lesson:
     ~
     ~   :e demo/lang-demo.scala<Enter>  enum, extension, @main -- Scala 3
     ~   :cd %:p:h                       the demo dir becomes the root
     ~   :!scala-cli setup-ide .         tell Metals how to build it
     ~   :CocRestart                     Metals imports (status: :CocInfo)
     ~
     ~  The first import downloads the compiler and can take a minute.
     ~  After that every key from steps 1-5 works unchanged: K on area,
     ~  gd on Shape, gr on describe, <leader>rn to rename a case, and
     ~  completion after "shapes." -- one muscle memory for every
     ~  language. Run the program from the editor:
     ~
     ~   :!scala-cli run .               compile + run, output below
     ~   :term scala-cli repl .          a REPL with the demo loaded
     ~
     ~ NOTE: Scala files indent by 2 (community style). In Neovim the
     ~ tree-sitter "scala" parser highlights them (:TSInstall scala if
     ~ :PlugInstall ran before Scala support existed); Vim uses
     ~ vim-polyglot. sbt/Mill projects work too: open a file inside the
     ~ project and Metals offers to import the build.

  SUMMARY -- Lesson 12

  ~ Ctrl-Space Ctrl-N Ctrl-P Tab     trigger, choose, accept
  ~ K gd gr gy gi Ctrl-O             hover / definition / references / back
  ~ :CocList diagnostics ]g [g       see and traverse every problem
  ~ <Leader>rn <Leader>ac <Leader>qf  rename, act, quickfix
  ~ :CocList outline :CocCommand     structure and the command palette
  ~ :CocInstall :CocConfig :CocInfo  extend, configure, troubleshoot
  ~ setup.sh --with-scala            Scala 3: Metals + scala-cli, step 7

================================================================================
